{
  "dom_id": "step-q353285",
  "name": "Question 5",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14220560/assets/e6cd07ff0c0f5b1b092e6501f41e9f61f3f98e794b5075461acfb1e97309cb32.png)\n\nFind the total finite area $A$ enclosed between the curves $y=15{x}^{4}+1$ and $y=15{x}^{2}+1,$ as shown above.  Do not round the answer.\n\n$A=$\n\n{{field-1}}",
  "worked_solution": "Let $f(x)=15{x}^{4}+1$ and $g(x)=15{x}^{2}+1.$ First, we find the intersections of the curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & f(x)=g(x) \\\\  & 15{x}^{4}+1=15{x}^{2}+1 \\\\  & 15{x}^{4}=15{x}^{2} \\\\  & 15{x}^{4}-15{x}^{2}=0 \\\\  & 15{x}^{2}({x}^{2}-1)=0 \\\\ x & =-1,0,1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe area we want consists of two regions: ${A}_{1}$ and ${A}_{2}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14220560/assets/66ae463184e8d5fc9f667019be7fc5c893aea736a6d0dad8fa6a2619df9f08e8.png)\n\n- For the area ${A}_{1},$ we have $x\\in [-1,0],$ and the upper function is $g(x)$ while the lower function is $f(x).$ So, the area is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}(g(x)-f(x))\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}((15{x}^{2}+1)-(15{x}^{4}+1))\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}(15{x}^{2}-15{x}^{4})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={(5{x}^{3}-3{x}^{5})|}_{-1}^{0} \\\\  & =0-(5(-1)^{3}-3(-1)^{5}) \\\\  & =0-(-5+3) \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For the area ${A}_{2},$ we have $x\\in [0,1],$ and the upper function is $g(x)$ while the lower function is $f(x).$ So, the area is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(g(x)-f(x))\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}((15{x}^{2}+1)-(15{x}^{4}+1))\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(15{x}^{2}-15{x}^{4})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={(5{x}^{3}-3{x}^{5})|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =(5-3)-0 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the required area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={A}_{1}+{A}_{2}=2+2=4.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
